// Rust Codex, part 2: Modules → Testing. Entries unlock when their challenge is solved.
[
    (
        id: "codex_modules_visibility",
        topic: "modules",
        title: "Privacy and pub",
        summary: "Everything is private to its module by default; `pub` opens it up one level at a time.",
        body: r##"Items (functions, structs, constants, modules) are private by default: only the module they are defined in, and its children, can use them. Mark an item `pub` to expose it to the parent and beyond.

A struct can be public while its fields stay private — that is how you hide representation and enforce invariants through constructors and methods. Make a field `pub` only when any value of that type is valid. Variants of a public enum are always public.

Finer-grained forms exist: `pub(crate)` (visible inside this crate only) and `pub(super)` (visible to the parent module)."##,
        example: r##"mod engine {
    pub struct Counter { value: u32 }      // field is private
    impl Counter {
        pub fn new() -> Counter { Counter { value: 0 } }
        pub fn bump(&mut self) { self.value += 1; }
        pub fn get(&self) -> u32 { self.value }
    }
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch07-02-defining-modules-to-control-scope-and-privacy.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("modules_01_visibility"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_modules_paths",
        topic: "modules",
        title: "Paths and the use keyword",
        summary: "`crate::`, `self::` and `super::` locate items; `use` brings them into scope.",
        body: r##"An item is named by a path through the module tree. `crate::audio::play` starts at the crate root (absolute); `self::` starts at the current module and `super::` at the parent (relative).

`use` shortens paths: `use std::collections::HashMap;`. Bring a function's PARENT module into scope (`use crate::audio;` then `audio::play()`) so the origin stays visible, but import types and traits directly. Group imports with braces (`use std::{fmt, io};`), rename with `as`, and re-export with `pub use` to shape your public API independently of your file layout."##,
        example: r##"mod audio {
    pub mod mixer { pub fn volume() -> u8 { 7 } }
    pub fn play() -> u8 { self::mixer::volume() }
}
use audio::mixer;
assert_eq!(mixer::volume(), audio::play());"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch07-04-bringing-paths-into-scope-with-the-use-keyword.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("modules_03_predict_paths"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_modules_crates",
        topic: "modules",
        title: "Packages, crates and workspaces",
        summary: "A package contains crates; a crate is a tree of modules; a workspace groups packages.",
        body: r##"A CRATE is the unit the compiler builds: a binary (has `main`) or a library. A PACKAGE is what Cargo manages — a `Cargo.toml` with at least one crate. Modules are the namespaces inside a crate; each `mod name;` normally lives in `name.rs` or `name/mod.rs`.

A WORKSPACE shares one `Cargo.lock` and one `target` directory between several packages — this game is one: `studio-core` is a library, the app depends on it. Dependencies are declared in `Cargo.toml` and resolved from crates.io; features turn optional parts on and off."##,
        example: r##"# Cargo.toml of a workspace
[workspace]
members = ["crates/studio-core", "crates/studio-app"]"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch14-03-cargo-workspaces.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("modules_04_nested_privacy"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_traits_basics",
        topic: "traits",
        title: "Traits: shared behaviour",
        summary: "A trait is a set of methods a type promises to provide; default methods come for free.",
        body: r##"A trait declares behaviour: method signatures, optionally with default bodies. A type opts in with `impl Trait for Type`. Implementors write the required methods and inherit the defaults, which can still be overridden.

Traits are Rust's tool for polymorphism and code reuse (there is no inheritance). The standard library is built from them: `Display`, `Clone`, `Iterator`, `From`, `Add`, `Default`, … Because the "orphan rule" says you may implement a trait only if the trait or the type is local to your crate, impls can never conflict between crates."##,
        example: r##"trait Renderable {
    fn glyph(&self) -> char;
    fn render(&self) -> String {              // default method
        self.glyph().to_string()
    }
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-02-traits.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("traits_01_renderable"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_traits_generics",
        topic: "traits",
        title: "Generics and trait bounds",
        summary: "Write the function once for every type that has the capabilities you need.",
        body: r##"Generics (`fn largest<T>(items: &[T]) -> &T`) let one definition work for many types. Inside the body the compiler knows nothing about `T`, so you say what you need with trait bounds: `T: PartialOrd + Copy`, or in a `where` clause for readability.

Rust compiles a separate, specialised version for each concrete type (MONOMORPHISATION), so generics are as fast as hand-written code — you pay in compile time and binary size, not at run time. `impl Trait` in argument position is shorthand for an anonymous generic; in return position it means "one concrete type that implements the trait"."##,
        example: r##"fn strongest<T: PartialOrd>(units: &[T]) -> Option<&T> {
    let mut best = units.first()?;
    for u in units { if u > best { best = u; } }
    Some(best)
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-01-syntax.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("traits_02_generic_strongest"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_traits_dyn",
        topic: "traits",
        title: "Trait objects: dyn Trait",
        summary: "Store different types behind one interface, at the price of a vtable call.",
        body: r##"A generic function is monomorphised per type; a list of mixed types needs DYNAMIC dispatch. `Box<dyn Weapon>` (or `&dyn Weapon`) is a fat pointer: the address of the value plus a pointer to a vtable of that type's methods. Each call looks the method up at run time.

Use trait objects when the set of types is open or decided at run time (plugins, heterogeneous collections, GUI widgets). Use generics when you want speed and one type at a time. Only OBJECT-SAFE traits can be made into objects: no methods returning `Self`, no generic methods."##,
        example: r##"let bag: Vec<Box<dyn Weapon>> = vec![Box::new(Sword), Box::new(Bow { arrows: 4 })];
let total: u32 = bag.iter().map(|w| w.damage()).sum();"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch18-02-trait-objects.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("traits_03_weapon_objects"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_lifetimes_annotations",
        topic: "lifetimes",
        title: "Lifetime annotations",
        summary: "`'a` names how long a reference is valid, so the compiler can relate inputs to outputs.",
        body: r##"Every reference has a lifetime — the region of code where it is valid. Usually the compiler infers it. When a function returns a reference derived from more than one input, you must say which: `fn pick<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str`.

An annotation never changes how long a value lives; it DESCRIBES the relationship so the compiler can verify that the result is not used after one of its sources is gone. If the compiler asks for a lifetime, ask yourself: "which argument does the returned reference point into?""##,
        example: r##"fn pick_longer<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if a.len() >= b.len() { a } else { b }
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-03-lifetime-syntax.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("lifetimes_01_longer_name"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_lifetimes_structs",
        topic: "lifetimes",
        title: "Structs that borrow",
        summary: "A struct holding a reference needs a lifetime parameter — and gives you zero-copy parsing.",
        body: r##"If a struct field is a reference, the struct needs a lifetime parameter: `struct Token<'a> { text: &'a str }`. It says "a Token cannot outlive the string it points into" and the compiler enforces it wherever tokens are used.

The payoff is performance: a parser that returns `&str` slices of the input allocates nothing per token. When the data does not live long enough, or the struct must be stored independently, own it (`String`) instead — borrowing is an optimisation, not an obligation."##,
        example: r##"struct Token<'a> { text: &'a str, line: usize }

fn tokenize(src: &str) -> Vec<Token<'_>> {
    src.split_whitespace().map(|text| Token { text, line: 1 }).collect()
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-03-lifetime-syntax.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("lifetimes_02_tokens"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_lifetimes_elision",
        topic: "lifetimes",
        title: "Lifetime elision",
        summary: "Three rules let the compiler fill in the obvious lifetimes so you rarely write them.",
        body: r##"You can often leave lifetimes out because of three elision rules: (1) each reference parameter gets its own lifetime; (2) if there is exactly ONE input lifetime, the output uses it; (3) if one parameter is `&self` or `&mut self`, outputs use the lifetime of `self`.

`fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str` therefore means `fn first_word<'a>(s: &'a str) -> &'a str`. When elision cannot decide (two reference parameters, no `self`) you get error E0106 and must annotate. `'static` is the lifetime of data that lives for the whole program, such as string literals."##,
        example: r##"fn first_word(s: &str) -> &str { s.split(' ').next().unwrap_or("") }
fn label() -> &'static str { "ready" }       // string literal: 'static"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch10-03-lifetime-syntax.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("lifetimes_03_review_dangling"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_iterators_closures",
        topic: "iterators",
        title: "Closures",
        summary: "Anonymous functions that capture their environment by reference, mutable reference or move.",
        body: r##"A closure is written `|args| body` and can use variables from the surrounding scope. The compiler picks the lightest capture: shared borrow if it only reads, mutable borrow if it modifies, by value if it consumes. `move |…|` forces by-value capture — required when a closure must outlive the function that made it, such as a thread.

Closures implement `Fn` (can be called many times, only reads), `FnMut` (can mutate captured state) or `FnOnce` (may consume captured values, callable once). Functions taking closures declare which they need: `fn apply(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32)`."##,
        example: r##"let mut clicks = 0;
let mut record = || clicks += 1;   // captures `clicks` by &mut
record();
record();
assert_eq!(clicks, 2);"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch13-01-closures.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("iterators_01_closure_borrow"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_iterators_adapters",
        topic: "iterators",
        title: "Iterator adapters and consumers",
        summary: "Lazy adapters (`map`, `filter`) describe a pipeline; consumers (`sum`, `collect`) run it.",
        body: r##"Iterators are lazy: `map`, `filter`, `take`, `skip`, `zip`, `enumerate`, `rev`, `chain`, `windows`/`chunks` (on slices) only build a new iterator and do nothing until a CONSUMER pulls items: `collect`, `sum`, `count`, `min`/`max`, `fold`, `any`/`all`, `find`, `for` loops.

Iterator chains compile to the same machine code as hand-written loops (zero-cost abstraction) and are usually easier to get right: no index variables, no off-by-one. `iter()` borrows items, `iter_mut()` borrows them mutably and `into_iter()` consumes the collection."##,
        example: r##"let total: u32 = hits
    .iter()
    .filter(|(_, crit)| *crit)
    .map(|(damage, _)| damage * 2)
    .sum();"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch13-02-iterators.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("iterators_02_loop_to_chain"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_iterators_custom",
        topic: "iterators",
        title: "Implementing Iterator",
        summary: "Write `next` and your type works with `for` and every adapter in the standard library.",
        body: r##"The `Iterator` trait needs just an `Item` type and `fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>`. Return `Some(item)` while there is more and `None` when finished. All the adapters and consumers are default methods built on `next`.

Implement `IntoIterator` for your collection types so that `for x in &collection` works, and remember that iterators are STATE MACHINES — that is why `next` takes `&mut self`. `std::iter::from_fn` and `std::iter::successors` create iterators from closures without a new type."##,
        example: r##"struct Wave { spawned: u32, count: u32 }
impl Iterator for Wave {
    type Item = u32;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {
        if self.spawned == self.count { return None; }
        self.spawned += 1;
        Some(10 * self.spawned)
    }
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch13-02-iterators.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("iterators_03_wave_iterator"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_smart_box",
        topic: "smart_pointers",
        title: "Box<T>: owning heap data",
        summary: "One owner, one heap allocation — essential for recursive types and trait objects.",
        body: r##"`Box<T>` puts a value on the heap and owns it; dropping the box frees it. A box is exactly one pointer wide, which makes it the standard way to build types whose size would otherwise be infinite (linked lists, trees, ASTs) and to hold trait objects (`Box<dyn Trait>`).

`Box<T>` implements `Deref`, so you can use it almost like the value itself. Moving a big struct into a `Box` is also a cheap way to pass it around without memcpy-ing the whole thing."##,
        example: r##"enum Tree {
    Leaf(i32),
    Node(Box<Tree>, Box<Tree>),
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch15-01-box.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("smart_pointers_01_boxed_dialogue"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_smart_rc",
        topic: "smart_pointers",
        title: "Rc<T> and Arc<T>: shared ownership",
        summary: "Several owners, freed when the last one goes away — `Rc` for one thread, `Arc` for many.",
        body: r##"`Rc<T>` (reference counted) lets multiple parts of a program own the same value. `Rc::clone(&x)` only increments a counter; the value is dropped when the last `Rc` disappears. `Rc::strong_count` shows the number of owners and `Rc::ptr_eq` tells whether two handles point to the same allocation.

`Rc` is single-threaded — its counter is not atomic, so it is not `Send`. Use `Arc<T>` to share across threads. Both give only SHARED access to the contents; to mutate shared data combine them with interior mutability: `Rc<RefCell<T>>` or `Arc<Mutex<T>>`. Beware cycles — use `Weak<T>` for back-references."##,
        example: r##"use std::rc::Rc;
let tex = Rc::new(String::from("grass.png"));
let a = Rc::clone(&tex);
assert_eq!(Rc::strong_count(&tex), 2);
drop(a);
assert_eq!(Rc::strong_count(&tex), 1);"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch15-04-rc.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("smart_pointers_02_shared_textures"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_smart_refcell",
        topic: "smart_pointers",
        title: "RefCell<T>: interior mutability",
        summary: "Borrowing rules checked at run time — mutation through a shared reference, with a panic if you break them.",
        body: r##"`RefCell<T>` lets you mutate a value through `&RefCell<T>`. Instead of the compiler, the cell checks the borrowing rules at RUN TIME: `borrow()` gives a shared guard, `borrow_mut()` an exclusive one, and breaking the rules (two `borrow_mut` at once) panics with `BorrowMutError`. `try_borrow_mut` returns a `Result` instead.

Use it sparingly: ordinary `&mut` is checked for free and cannot panic. The classic combination `Rc<RefCell<T>>` is the single-threaded way to have shared, mutable data (graphs, GUI trees). `Cell<T>` is the lighter option for small `Copy` values."##,
        example: r##"use std::cell::RefCell;
let chest = RefCell::new(50);
*chest.borrow_mut() += 5;
assert_eq!(*chest.borrow(), 55);"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch15-05-interior-mutability.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("smart_pointers_03_refcell_transfer"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_testing_unit",
        topic: "testing",
        title: "Unit tests",
        summary: "`#[test]` functions in a `#[cfg(test)]` module, run with `cargo test`.",
        body: r##"A test is a function marked `#[test]`; it passes unless it panics. Put unit tests in a `mod tests` annotated `#[cfg(test)]` at the bottom of the file, with `use super::*;` to reach the code under test — the module only exists in test builds.

Assert with `assert!(cond)`, `assert_eq!(left, right)` and `assert_ne!`; add a message as the last argument. `cargo test` runs tests in parallel threads and captures their output (use `-- --nocapture` to see it, `-- --test-threads=1` to serialise). Filter by name with `cargo test name_part`."##,
        example: r##"#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn caps_at_max() {
        assert_eq!(clamp_health(150, 100), 100);
    }
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch11-01-writing-tests.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("testing_01_clamp_with_tests"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_testing_panics",
        topic: "testing",
        title: "Testing failures: should_panic and Result",
        summary: "Test that bad input fails loudly, and use `?` inside tests by returning `Result`.",
        body: r##"`#[should_panic(expected = "text")]` makes a test pass only if the code panics AND the message contains the text — so an unrelated panic cannot sneak through. Document such behaviour in a `# Panics` section of the function's doc comment.

A test may also return `Result<(), E>`: it passes on `Ok(())` and fails on `Err`, which lets you use `?` instead of `unwrap()` and get the real error in the failure output. (You cannot combine that with `#[should_panic]`.) Mark slow or unfinished tests `#[ignore]` and run them with `cargo test -- --ignored`."##,
        example: r##"#[test]
#[should_panic(expected = "no frames")]
fn empty_input_panics() {
    average_frame_time(&[]);
}

#[test]
fn parses() -> Result<(), std::num::ParseIntError> {
    assert_eq!(parse_level(" 7 ")?, 7);
    Ok(())
}"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch11-01-writing-tests.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("testing_02_should_panic"),
    ),
    (
        id: "codex_testing_organisation",
        topic: "testing",
        title: "Doc comments, doc tests and integration tests",
        summary: "`///` comments become documentation whose code examples are tested; `tests/` holds black-box tests.",
        body: r##"Triple-slash comments (`///`) document the item below them in Markdown; `//!` documents the enclosing module or crate. Code blocks inside doc comments are compiled and run by `cargo test` as DOC TESTS, so your examples can never go stale. Conventional sections are `# Examples`, `# Panics`, `# Errors` and `# Safety`.

Integration tests live in the top-level `tests/` directory; each file is compiled as a separate crate that can use only your PUBLIC API — they test the library the way a user sees it. `cargo test --doc`, `--lib` and `--test name` select which kind to run."##,
        example: r##"/// Adds two numbers.
///
/// # Examples
/// ```
/// assert_eq!(my_crate::add(2, 3), 5);
/// ```
pub fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 { a + b }"##,
        book_url: "https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch11-03-test-organization.html",
        unlock_challenge: Some("testing_03_result_test"),
    ),
]
